{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# RCA — Phase 1 complete. The effect is REAL, but `nohup` is not the cause.\n\nInvestigated in an isolated worktree off `origin/main` (`987caf8c73`), `tests/e2e/js` warmed\n(`npm install` + `npm run build`), Chromium verified launching. Machine load average 9-13\nthroughout (this host runs several agents), i.e. the contention the ticket names as a candidate\nwas present in every run below.\n\n## Finding 1 — detachment ALONE does not reproduce it (0 / 15 runs)\n\nExact invocations, both from the same worktree:\n\n    # foreground\n    env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest \\\n      tests/e2e/test_editor_browser.py tests/integration/test_reclaim_bridge_history.py -q\n\n    # detached, stdin at /dev/null, no controlling terminal (as reported)\n    nohup env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest \\\n      tests/e2e/test_editor_browser.py tests/integration/test_reclaim_bridge_history.py -q \\\n      > /tmp/log 2>&1 < /dev/null &\n\nResults:\n- foreground, 7-test subset: **7 passed / 22.6s**\n- detached, same subset: **7 passed / 22.7s** (identical)\n- detached, full 12-test selection, N=6 consecutive: **12 passed every time**\n  (27.9 / 28.6 / 26.9 / 27.8 / 27.x / 27.x s)\n- detached `pytest tests/e2e` (warm): **12 passed** — the exact number the report cites\n- three CONCURRENT detached `pytest tests/e2e` + one `test_reclaim_bridge_history.py`,\n  3 rounds (8 e2e runs + 2 integration runs, uninterfered): **all clean, 0 failures**\n\nSo the absent TTY, `stdin=/dev/null`, the process session, and resource contention are all\n**rejected** as causes. \"Detached runs are untrustworthy\" is not a property of detachment.\n\nAlso worth recording, because it makes the cold path safe: with `tests/e2e/js/node_modules`\nabsent (the state of a FRESH worktree, which `AGENTS.md` mandates), `pytest tests/e2e` reports\n**4 passed, 8 skipped** — the tier self-skips, exactly as `tests/e2e/conftest.py`'s module\ndocstring promises. It does not error.\n\n## Finding 2 — the mechanism that DOES reproduce it: a concurrent external mutation of the\n## shared checkout, misattributed by an autouse guard to an innocent test\n\n`tests/conftest.py:297-325` `_no_repo_root_leaks` is an autouse fixture that snapshots the\ntop-level entries of `REPO_ROOT` before each test and diffs them after. It cannot tell an\nin-test mutation from an external one.\n\nDISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT (deterministic, 1/1):\n\n    # detached run of the implicated integration file; from a separate shell,\n    # 4s in, a single external touch — nothing the tests do:\n    touch /Users/joeoakhart/debug-f0fb-detached-pytest/EXTERNAL_MUTATION_PROBE\n\nObserved:\n\n    E  Failed: Test leaked new entries into REPO_ROOT (use tmp_path or a sandboxed temp dir):\n       ['EXTERNAL_MUTATION_PROBE']\n    tests/conftest.py:322: Failed\n    ERROR tests/integration/test_reclaim_bridge_history.py::test_cli_never_invokes_push_and_leaves_source_refs_exact\n    6 passed, 1 error in 7.55s\n\nThree things to note, all of which match the original report exactly:\n\n1. **`6 passed, 1 error`** — the guard fires in TEARDOWN, so the test still counts as passed\n   AND raises an error. \"It errored, then on re-run the file's 6 passed\" is not two\n   contradictory observations; it is one run reporting both. (Verified independently with a\n   throwaway autouse teardown-fail fixture: pytest renders it `1 passed, 1 error`.)\n2. **It blamed an innocent test.** `test_cli_never_invokes_push_and_leaves_source_refs_exact`\n   did nothing wrong. The guard attributes to whichever test happened to straddle the external\n   write — arbitrary, and different on each run. Same signature the HEAD-move sibling has.\n3. **The message asserts a falsehood** — \"Test leaked new entries into REPO_ROOT\" — when no\n   test leaked anything. And the guard **deleted the external file** (`tests/conftest.py:316`\n   `target.unlink()`): the probe was gone afterwards. A detached suite will silently delete a\n   new top-level file an agent creates while it runs.\n\n## Finding 3 — the second mechanism: a signal-killed child probe reports NO cause at all\n\nThe report's own session records \"tests/interfaces clean through 59% before **the runner was\nreaped**\". Reaping is therefore established for that session. What a reap does to the browser\ntier (deterministic, 4/4 — `kill -9` on the `node` probe child mid-test):\n\n    E  AssertionError: browser_diag.mjs produced no output; stderr:\n    tests/e2e/conftest.py:106: AssertionError\n\n`tests/e2e/conftest.py:100-109` raises on empty stdout and prints `proc.stderr` — which is\nEMPTY when the child died on a signal — and **never inspects `proc.returncode`** (it is `-9`\nhere). The resulting message names no cause whatsoever, and is indistinguishable from a real\nproduct regression. That is precisely why the reporter \"nearly chased those as real\nregressions\".\n\nNote this also matters for `nohup`: `nohup` ignores SIGHUP but does NOT `setsid`. Measured on\nthis host, a `nohup ... &` child has the SAME pgid as the launching shell, so every `git` and\n`node` subprocess these fixtures spawn is reapable by one `kill -- -PGID`.\n\n## Why this looked like \"detached vs foreground\"\n\nBoth mechanisms need an EXTERNAL actor running concurrently with the suite. A foreground run\nstructurally prevents that — the agent is blocked waiting on it. A detached run exists\nprecisely so the agent can keep working in the same checkout, so it is the only mode in which\na concurrent `git commit`, branch switch, file creation, or reap can land mid-run. Detachment\nis an ENABLER of the real cause, not the cause. That distinction is the whole ticket: the\nremedy is not \"distrust detached runs\", it is \"do not mutate the checkout while a suite runs\nin it, and make the guards say so when someone does\".\n\n## Separating this from the KNOWN self-inflicted HEAD-move class (AC3)\n\nThey are siblings, not the same thing, and both are external-mutation misattributions:\n- `_no_repo_commits` (`tests/conftest.py:610-633`) — \"Test moved the repo HEAD (X -> Y)\".\n  Already understood; fires on a concurrent commit/rebase/branch switch.\n- `_no_repo_root_leaks` (`tests/conftest.py:297-325`) — \"Test leaked new entries into\n  REPO_ROOT\". The one this report actually hit, and the one that had NOT been named. It fires\n  on any concurrent top-level write, and additionally DELETES what it finds.\n\nThe reason the report read as a new mystery is that only the HEAD-move sibling was folklore.\nA real precedent for the leak guard exists in a detached full-suite log on this host from the\nprevious day: `14671 passed, 55 skipped, 4 errors in 1:10:59`, all four errors\n`Failed: Test leaked new entries into REPO_ROOT`, on four unrelated unit tests.\n\n## Prior art — this guard firing on a NON-TEST cause is already a filed-and-fixed bug class\n\n- `3a03-9863-542e-4ae1` (`hurt-brow-swan`, closed) — `_no_repo_root_leaks` blind to leaks into\n  pre-existing top-level dirs.\n- `f8a2-bf40-4cc4-479b` (`dizzy-splice-coven`, closed) — \"bash-suite REPO_ROOT leak\n  (.tickets-tracker) + **macOS tmp-cleaner race**\". A non-test external actor tripping this\n  guard, filed and fixed as a bug.\n- Ticket `5ac8` records another: \"1 environmental teardown artifact (.tickets-tracker symlink\n  flagged by the REPO_ROOT leak detector ...; the test passes in isolation\".\n\nSo \"the leak guard reports an environmental cause as a test defect\" is established in this\nproject as a defect, not as intended behaviour.\n\n## Defect origin (`caused_by`) — UNATTRIBUTABLE, with the searches\n\nThe implicated lines are the `pytest.fail` message and the `unlink`/`rmtree` cleanup,\n`tests/conftest.py:311-325`. `git blame -w -C -M -L 305,325` puts all of them on\n**`b534f47a7f9`** (2026-06-08), \"Extract DSO ticket system + Jira reconciler into standalone\nrebar\" — the commit that first added `tests/conftest.py`\n(`git log --diff-filter=A -- tests/conftest.py` returns only that commit). It carries **no\n`rebar-ticket:` trailer** (only a `Co-Authored-By:`); it predates the trailer convention.\n\nDate test: the guard has behaved this way since the file's first commit, so this is an\nOMISSION (it was never written to consider a concurrent external mutator), not a regression —\nthere is no commit that made it wrong. Tracker searches for the originating work\n(`rebar search \"repo isolation guard leak REPO_ROOT\"`, `rebar search \"local-dev-env whole tree\nvalidation detached\"`) return only later bugs ABOUT the guard (`3a03`, `f8a2`), not the work\nthat introduced it. Recording as **unattributable** rather than charging it to the mass\nextraction import or to one of the later bugs that merely widened it.\n\n## What is left, and why it is an OPERATOR DECISION rather than something I landed\n\nAC5 as written (\"if specific tests genuinely cannot run detached, they SKIP\") rests on a\npremise this investigation DISPROVES: no test here is unable to run detached — all 15 detached\nruns were clean. Adding a skip keyed on detachment would suppress a signal for a cause that\ndoes not exist, which is the \"erosion of the signal\" this ticket was filed to prevent.\n\nThe remedies the evidence actually supports, none of which I applied unilaterally because each\nchanges a safety guard or the project's documented method:\n\n1. **`docs/local-dev-env.md` (AC4) — a genuine, verified gap.** `grep -rn -i\n   'nohup|detached|background'` over `docs/`, `AGENTS.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `Makefile`\n   returns ZERO hits. The detached-suite practice is entirely undocumented folklore. The\n   recorded guidance should be: detached runs ARE trustworthy, provided nothing else touches\n   the checkout while they run; run them from a DEDICATED worktree; and prefer\n   `-n <cores> --dist worksteal --timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread -p no:cacheprovider\n   --basetemp=...` (what CI uses — `pytest-xdist` and `pytest-timeout` are already pinned in\n   `[dev]` for local<->CI parity but are NOT enabled by `addopts`, so a bare local run is\n   serial with no hang guard).\n2. **`tests/conftest.py:322-325`** — stop asserting \"Test leaked\"; say the entry MAY come from\n   a concurrent process outside the test, and name that possibility. Purely additive to the\n   message; weakens no detection.\n3. **`tests/conftest.py:310-321`** — reconsider deleting entries the guard cannot attribute.\n   Today a detached suite destroys a concurrent agent's new top-level file.\n4. **`tests/e2e/conftest.py:106-109`** — include `proc.returncode` in the diagnostic. A `-9`\n   or `-15` tells a reader instantly that the child was killed rather than that the product\n   broke. Additive; changes no pass/fail outcome.\n\n(2) and (4) are additive-diagnostic and low risk. (3) genuinely weakens a cleanup that exists\nfor a reason. (1) is the ticket's headline deliverable. All four want an operator's call on\nscope before they land, so I am stopping Phase 1 here rather than patching a safety guard on\nmy own judgement."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787259315837907001,"uuid":"9609c552-12f0-4849-aeee-4a07fc814dc4"}